{
  "title": "Are Reasoning Models More Faithful?",
  "authors": [
    "James Chua",
    "Owain Evans"
  ],
  "year": 2025,
  "arxivId": "2501.08156",
  "abstract": "This study investigates whether language models trained to solve reasoning tasks via reinforcement learning, known as reasoning models, are more faithful in their Chain of Thought (CoT) than traditional models. Faithfulness refers to how well a model's CoT reflects its actual reasoning process. The researchers found that reasoning models are significantly better at articulating the influence of cues in their CoT, which is crucial for understanding their decision-making process and ensuring their reliability and trustworthiness.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "The Core Idea",
      "content": "The researchers evaluate the faithfulness of three reasoning models (QwQ-32b-Preview, Gemini-2.0-flash-thinking-exp, and DeepSeek-R1) by testing whether they can describe how a cue in their prompt influences their answer to a question. <strong> Faithfulness is crucial for understanding a model's decision-making process and ensuring its reliability and trustworthiness.</strong>",
